PART B – AWS EC2 UBUNTU HANDS-ON LABS

DAY 1: Deploy Apache Kafka on AWS EC2 Using Docker

Main Topic: AWS EC2, Ubuntu, Docker Engine, and Apache Kafka Deployment

Hands-on:
Launch an Ubuntu EC2 instance, install Docker, deploy Apache Kafka 4.4.0, create topics, publish messages, consume messages, and inspect consumer groups.

--------------------------------------------------

7. LAB ENVIRONMENT ARCHITECTURE

We will deploy Kafka on one Ubuntu EC2 instance using Docker.

Architecture:

Trainer's Laptop
       |
       |
SSH Client / Terminal
       |
       |
SSH Port 22
       |
       |
AWS Cloud
       |
       |
Amazon EC2
       |
       |
Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS
       |
       |
Docker Engine
       |
       |
Apache Kafka 4.4.0
       |
       |
Single-node KRaft Broker + Controller
       |
       |
Port 9092 – Local Access Only

Kafka CLI producer and consumer commands run inside the Docker container.

AWS Infrastructure Requirements:

Cloud: AWS

Service: Amazon EC2

Operating System: Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS

Instance Type: t3.medium or larger

CPU: 2 vCPU

RAM: 4 GiB

Storage: 20 GiB gp3 EBS

Security Group:
- SSH port 22 from trainer's public IP only.
- No inbound Kafka port 9092.

Kafka Version: Apache Kafka 4.4.0

Container Runtime: Docker Engine

Region: Any suitable AWS Region.

Note:

For a smoother training session with additional applications, an 8 GiB instance such as t3.large is preferable, although it costs more.

Security:

Do not open TCP port 9092 to 0.0.0.0/0.

All Kafka commands in this handbook execute inside the Docker container, so public Kafka access is unnecessary.

Kafka's default development configuration is not appropriate for an Internet-exposed broker.

--------------------------------------------------

8. LAB 1 – LAUNCH AWS EC2

Estimated Time: 10 Minutes

Step 1: Open AWS Console

Visit the AWS Management Console:

https://console.aws.amazon.com/

Navigate to:

AWS Console → EC2 → Instances → Launch Instance

Step 2: Configure the EC2 Instance

Use the following configuration:

Name: kafka-day1-training

AMI: Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS

Architecture: x86_64

Instance Type: t3.medium

Key Pair: kafka-training-key

Storage: 20 GiB gp3

Public IP: Enabled

Select an appropriate VPC and public subnet with Internet connectivity.

Step 3: Configure Security Group

Create or select a security group with the following inbound rule:

Type: SSH

Protocol: TCP

Port: 22

Source: My IP /32

Keep outbound access available for package installation and pulling Docker images.

Important:

Do not add inbound port 9092.

Step 4: Launch and Connect to EC2

Once the instance is running, copy its Public IPv4 Address or Public DNS.

From your local terminal, use the private key to connect.

Set private key permissions:

```bash
chmod 400 kafka-training-key.pem
```

Connect to EC2:

```bash
ssh -i kafka-training-key.pem \
ubuntu@YOUR_EC2_PUBLIC_IP
```

Replace YOUR_EC2_PUBLIC_IP with your actual EC2 public IP address.

Expected Terminal Prompt:

```text
ubuntu@ip-172-31-xx-xx:~$
```

EC2 Explanation:

EC2 provides the virtual machine.

Ubuntu is the operating system.

Docker runs Kafka inside a container.

Kafka is the event-streaming service.

SSH provides administrative access.

--------------------------------------------------

9. LAB 2 – PREPARE UBUNTU SERVER

Run all the following commands on the EC2 instance.

Step 1: Check Operating System Details

```bash
cat /etc/os-release
```

Expected relevant output:

```text
PRETTY_NAME="Ubuntu 24.04.x LTS"
```

Step 2: Check CPU and Memory

Check available CPU cores:

```bash
nproc
```

Check memory:

```bash
free -h
```

Check disk space:

```bash
df -h /
```

Explain:

nproc shows available logical CPUs.

free -h shows available memory.

df -h / shows filesystem storage space.

Step 3: Update Package Metadata and Installed Packages

```bash
sudo apt-get update
```

Upgrade installed packages:

```bash
sudo apt-get upgrade -y
```

Step 4: Install Required Utilities

```bash
sudo apt-get install -y \
ca-certificates \
curl \
gnupg \
git \
jq
```

Note:

jq is useful for working with JSON during later exercises.

--------------------------------------------------

10. LAB 3 – INSTALL DOCKER ENGINE

These steps follow Docker's official Ubuntu installation approach.

Official Documentation:

https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/ubuntu/

Step 1: Create the Keyring Directory

```bash
sudo install -m 0755 -d /etc/apt/keyrings
```

Step 2: Add Docker's Official Signing Key

```bash
sudo curl -fsSL \
https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg \
-o /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc

sudo chmod a+r /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc
```

Step 3: Add the Docker APT Repository

Copy the entire block:

```bash
sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.sources > /dev/null <<EOF
Types: deb
URIs: https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu
Suites: $(. /etc/os-release && echo "${UBUNTU_CODENAME:-$VERSION_CODENAME}")
Components: stable
Architectures: $(dpkg --print-architecture)
Signed-By: /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc
EOF
```

Step 4: Install Docker

```bash
sudo apt-get update

sudo apt-get install -y \
docker-ce \
docker-ce-cli \
containerd.io \
docker-buildx-plugin \
docker-compose-plugin
```

Step 5: Start and Enable Docker

```bash
sudo systemctl enable --now docker
```

Step 6: Verify Docker Installation

Check Docker version:

```bash
sudo docker --version
```

Check Docker service:

```bash
sudo systemctl is-active docker
```

Expected Output:

```text
Docker version ...
active
```

The version number depends on the installed Docker release.

Step 7: Run a Test Container

```bash
sudo docker run --rm hello-world
```

Expected Output Includes:

```text
Hello from Docker!

This message shows that your installation appears to be working correctly.
```

If this message appears, Docker installation is working.

Note:

For this course, we will continue using sudo docker so that no Docker user-group changes are required.

--------------------------------------------------

11. LAB 4 – DOWNLOAD AND START KAFKA

Estimated Time: 10–15 Minutes

We will use the official JVM-based Apache Kafka Docker image, version 4.4.0.

Step 1: Pull Kafka Docker Image

```bash
sudo docker pull apache/kafka:4.4.0
```

Expected final output resembles:

```text
Status: Downloaded newer image for apache/kafka:4.4.0
```

If the image is already cached, Docker may report that it is up to date.

Step 2: Run Kafka Container

```bash
sudo docker run -d \
--name kafka-day1 \
--restart unless-stopped \
-p 127.0.0.1:9092:9092 \
apache/kafka:4.4.0
```

Command Explanation:

run: Creates and starts a container.

-d: Runs the container in detached mode.

--name: Gives the container a name.

--restart unless-stopped: Restarts the container after Docker daemon or host restarts unless explicitly stopped.

-p 127.0.0.1:9092:9092: Publishes port 9092 only on the EC2 loopback interface.

apache/kafka:4.4.0: Kafka container image.

Important:

The default image starts a development Kafka server with a KRaft configuration.

No separate ZooKeeper container is required.

Step 3: Check Container Status

```bash
sudo docker ps
```

Expected relevant output:

```text
IMAGE                NAMES
apache/kafka:4.4.0   kafka-day1
```

The container should show an Up status.

Step 4: View Kafka Logs

```bash
sudo docker logs --tail 50 kafka-day1
```

Look for startup completion and the absence of fatal errors.

Kafka may need some time to initialize.

Step 5: Verify Kafka CLI

```bash
sudo docker exec kafka-day1 \
/opt/kafka/bin/kafka-topics.sh \
--bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
--list
```

If the command succeeds without a connection error, the broker is responding.

An empty topic list is normal before creating topics.

Checkpoint 1: Kafka Is Operational

Students should demonstrate:

1. Running Docker container.
2. Successful Kafka topic-list command.
3. Kafka startup logs without fatal errors.

--------------------------------------------------

12. LAB 5 – CREATE A KAFKA TOPIC

Step 1: Create a Topic

Create a topic named student-events with three partitions.

```bash
sudo docker exec kafka-day1 \
/opt/kafka/bin/kafka-topics.sh \
--bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
--create \
--topic student-events \
--partitions 3 \
--replication-factor 1
```

Expected Output:

```text
Created topic student-events.
```

Step 2: List Topics

```bash
sudo docker exec kafka-day1 \
/opt/kafka/bin/kafka-topics.sh \
--bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
--list
```

Expected Output:

```text
student-events
```

Other internal topics may appear later.

Step 3: Describe the Topic

```bash
sudo docker exec kafka-day1 \
/opt/kafka/bin/kafka-topics.sh \
--bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
--describe \
--topic student-events
```

Illustrative Output:

```text
Topic: student-events
PartitionCount: 3
ReplicationFactor: 1

Partition: 0  Leader: 1  Replicas: 1  Isr: 1
Partition: 1  Leader: 1  Replicas: 1  Isr: 1
Partition: 2  Leader: 1  Replicas: 1  Isr: 1
```

The actual broker ID and formatting may differ.

Trainer Explanation:

Question 1: Why are all three partitions on the same broker?

Answer:

Because we have only one broker. Multiple partitions do not automatically mean multiple physical servers.

Question 2: Why is the replication factor 1?

Answer:

Because only one broker is available.

Replication factor 3 would require at least three brokers.

--------------------------------------------------

13. LAB 6 – SEND MESSAGES USING A KAFKA PRODUCER

Open a new SSH terminal to the same EC2 instance.

Step 1: Start the Kafka Console Producer

```bash
sudo docker exec -it kafka-day1 \
/opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-producer.sh \
--bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
--topic student-events
```

You should see an interactive prompt:

```text
>
```

Step 2: Enter Sample Messages

Enter the following messages, pressing Enter after each line.

```json
{"student_id":101,"name":"Rahul","course":"Kafka"}
{"student_id":102,"name":"Priya","course":"AWS"}
{"student_id":103,"name":"Arun","course":"DevOps"}
{"student_id":104,"name":"Meena","course":"Python"}
```

Explain What Happened:

1. The producer connects to the Kafka broker.
2. It discovers topic and partition metadata.
3. It sends records to the topic partitions.
4. Kafka appends accepted records to the partition logs.
5. The producer receives acknowledgments according to its configuration.

Important:

The console producer sends each entered line as a separate record.

Kafka does not automatically validate that the line is valid JSON.

--------------------------------------------------

14. LAB 7 – READ MESSAGES USING A KAFKA CONSUMER

Keep the producer running.

Open another SSH terminal and execute:

Step 1: Start the Kafka Console Consumer

```bash
sudo docker exec -it kafka-day1 \
/opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-consumer.sh \
--bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
--topic student-events \
--from-beginning
```

Expected Output Contains the Four Student Records:

```json
{"student_id":101,"name":"Rahul","course":"Kafka"}
{"student_id":102,"name":"Priya","course":"AWS"}
{"student_id":103,"name":"Arun","course":"DevOps"}
{"student_id":104,"name":"Meena","course":"Python"}
```

Note:

The display order may differ because the topic has three partitions.

Live Demonstration:

Return to the producer terminal and enter:

```json
{"student_id":105,"name":"Vahid","course":"Kubernetes"}
```

Observe the consumer terminal.

The new record should appear without restarting the consumer.

Trainer Explanation:

The producer and consumer are two independent clients.

The producer publishes records, and Kafka stores them.

The consumer reads records from Kafka.

The producer does not need to know which consumer will process the record.

Step 2: Demonstrate Message Replay

Stop the consumer using Ctrl+C.

Start a new consumer with a new group:

```bash
sudo docker exec -it kafka-day1 \
/opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-consumer.sh \
--bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
--topic student-events \
--group replay-demo \
--from-beginning
```

The new group can read retained messages from the beginning.

Explain:

Replay is possible because Kafka retains records according to retention policies.

Records are not normally deleted simply because a consumer reads them.

--------------------------------------------------

PART C – INTERMEDIATE CONCEPTS AND PRACTICAL EXERCISES

15. LAB 8 – OBSERVE PARTITIONS AND OFFSETS

For this lab, create a fresh single-partition topic to make ordering and offsets easy to understand.

Step 1: Create a Topic

```bash
sudo docker exec kafka-day1 \
/opt/kafka/bin/kafka-topics.sh \
--bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
--create \
--topic offset-demo \
--partitions 1 \
--replication-factor 1
```

Step 2: Produce Five Records

```bash
printf 'Message-A\nMessage-B\nMessage-C\nMessage-D\nMessage-E\n' | \
sudo docker exec -i kafka-day1 \
/opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-producer.sh \
--bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
--topic offset-demo
```

Step 3: Consume Messages With Offsets Displayed

```bash
sudo docker exec kafka-day1 \
/opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-consumer.sh \
--bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
--topic offset-demo \
--from-beginning \
--property print.partition=true \
--property print.offset=true \
--timeout-ms 10000
```

Illustrative Output:

```text
Partition:0 Offset:0 Message-A
Partition:0 Offset:1 Message-B
Partition:0 Offset:2 Message-C
Partition:0 Offset:3 Message-D
Partition:0 Offset:4 Message-E
```

The exact output formatting depends on the Kafka console formatter.

The consumer may print a timeout message after waiting for more records. That is expected for this finite demonstration.

Key Concept:

Single Partition Log

Message-A --> Offset 0

Message-B --> Offset 1

Message-C --> Offset 2

Message-D --> Offset 3

Message-E --> Offset 4

Records are appended in order.

The next offset would ordinarily be 5, even though no record currently exists at that position.

Trainer Question:

Why do offsets start at zero in this new topic?

Answer:

A new, empty partition normally starts with offset zero.

However, offsets in an existing log may not start at zero after retention or log truncation.

--------------------------------------------------

16. LAB 9 – CONSUMER GROUPS

This is one of the most important Kafka concepts.

Consumer Group Architecture:

Topic: group-demo

Partition 0
Partition 1
Partition 2

Consumer Group: training-group

Consumer 1
Consumer 2
Consumer 3

Illustrative balanced assignment:

Three consumers can each process one partition.

Actual assignments depend on the group protocol and assignor.

Step 1: Create a Topic for the Demonstration

```bash
sudo docker exec kafka-day1 \
/opt/kafka/bin/kafka-topics.sh \
--bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
--create \
--topic group-demo \
--partitions 3 \
--replication-factor 1
```

Step 2: Start Consumer 1

In SSH Terminal A:

```bash
sudo docker exec -it kafka-day1 \
/opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-consumer.sh \
--bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
--topic group-demo \
--group training-group
```

Step 3: Start Consumer 2

In SSH Terminal B, run the same command:

```bash
sudo docker exec -it kafka-day1 \
/opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-consumer.sh \
--bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
--topic group-demo \
--group training-group
```

Step 4: Produce Records

In SSH Terminal C:

```bash
sudo docker exec -it kafka-day1 \
/opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-producer.sh \
--bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
--topic group-demo
```

Enter the following messages:

```text
Order-1001
Order-1002
Order-1003
Order-1004
Order-1005
Order-1006
Order-1007
Order-1008
Order-1009
```

Expected Behavior:

The two consumers cooperate to process the topic's partitions.

Do not expect every consumer to receive an equal number of messages.

Distribution depends on partition assignment and which partitions receive records.

Step 5: Describe the Consumer Group

Open a fourth terminal:

```bash
sudo docker exec kafka-day1 \
/opt/kafka/bin/kafka-consumer-groups.sh \
--bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
--describe \
--group training-group
```

Output Includes Fields Such As:

```text
GROUP           TOPIC        PARTITION CURRENT-OFFSET LOG-END-OFFSET LAG
training-group  group-demo   0         ...            ...            ...
training-group  group-demo   1         ...            ...            ...
training-group  group-demo   2         ...            ...            ...
```

Explain These Terms:

Group:

Consumer group identifier.

Topic:

Topic being consumed.

Partition:

Partition number.

Current Offset:

Last committed consumer position, expressed as the next offset to consume.

Log-End Offset:

End position of the partition log.

Lag:

Difference between log-end offset and committed offset.

Note:

A consumer's committed offset may temporarily lag behind records already displayed because commits are not necessarily immediate.

Step 6: Add a Third Consumer

Open another SSH terminal and run the same group-consumer command.

After group assignment stabilizes, the three consumers can each receive one partition.

Step 7: Add a Fourth Consumer

Run a fourth consumer in the same group.

With three partitions and four consumers, at least one consumer will have no partition assignment for this topic.

Teaching Conclusion:

Adding consumers beyond the number of topic partitions does not increase the maximum parallelism for processing that topic within a single group.

--------------------------------------------------

PART D – KAFKA ARCHITECTURE: DEEPER EXPLANATION

17. MESSAGE LIFECYCLE

Explain this sequence before ending today's technical section.

Step 1: Producer Creates an Event

The application constructs a record with an optional key and value.

Step 2: Producer Selects a Partition

A partitioner uses the record key or configured partitioning behavior.

Step 3: Broker Appends the Record

The partition leader writes the record to its log.

Step 4: Replication Occurs

Follower replicas fetch data from the leader when replication is configured.

Step 5: Producer Receives Acknowledgment

The acknowledgment depends on settings such as acks and broker state.

Step 6: Consumer Fetches the Record

The consumer polls the broker for records assigned to it.

Step 7: Consumer Processes the Event

Application business logic executes.

Step 8: Consumer Commits Its Position

Offset commits track progress.

The exact timing depends on the application's commit strategy.

Important Clarification:

Kafka message consumption is not the same as message deletion.

Kafka generally retains records based on topic retention policies, irrespective of whether consumers have processed them.

Also, Kafka cannot guarantee that a consumer's external side effect, such as writing to a database, succeeds simply because the record was fetched.

--------------------------------------------------

18. KAFKA TERMINOLOGY REVISION

Producer: Message publisher.

Consumer: Message reader.

Broker: Kafka server.

Cluster: Collection of Kafka servers.

Topic: Named event stream.

Partition: Ordered event log.

Offset: Position in a partition.

Replica: Copy of partition.

Leader: Primary replica for reads and writes.

ISR: In-Sync Replicas.

Consumer Group: Cooperating consumers.

KRaft: Kafka metadata consensus mechanism.

Retention: How long or how much data Kafka keeps.

Lag: Consumer committed-position delay.

--------------------------------------------------

PART E – TROUBLESHOOTING GUIDE FOR LIVE TRAINING

19. COMMON KAFKA AND AWS EC2 ERRORS

Problem: SSH connection timed out.

Likely Cause:
Security group, network configuration, or wrong public IP.

Action:
Check SSH inbound rule and EC2 network reachability.

Problem: Permission denied (publickey).

Likely Cause:
Wrong private key or SSH username.

Action:
Use the correct PEM file and ubuntu username.

Problem: docker: command not found.

Likely Cause:
Docker is not installed.

Action:
Complete Docker installation.

Problem: Cannot connect to Docker daemon.

Likely Cause:
Docker service stopped or permission issue.

Action:

```bash
sudo systemctl start docker
```

Problem: Docker image pull fails.

Likely Cause:
No Internet connectivity, DNS issue, or registry access problem.

Action:
Check routing, DNS, and outbound HTTPS connectivity.

Problem: Kafka container exits.

Likely Cause:
Startup/configuration or resource problem.

Action:

```bash
sudo docker logs kafka-day1
```

Problem: Topic already exists.

Likely Cause:
Topic was created previously.

Action:
Use a new topic name or reuse the existing topic.

Problem: Replication factor larger than available brokers.

Likely Cause:
Too few brokers.

Action:
Use replication factor 1 for the single-broker lab.

Problem: Broker connection fails.

Likely Cause:
Kafka is not ready or the wrong address is being used.

Action:
Check docker ps, container logs, and port configuration.

Problem: Consumer shows no records.

Likely Cause:
Wrong topic, offset position, or group state.

Action:
Check the topic, producer, and consumer group offsets.

Problem: Consumer lag is nonzero.

Likely Cause:
Consumer is behind or commits are delayed.

Action:
Inspect group state and application behavior.

Problem: No space left on device.

Likely Cause:
EC2 EBS filesystem is full.

Action:
Check disk usage and expand storage if required.

20. ESSENTIAL DIAGNOSTIC COMMANDS

Check EC2 system health:

```bash
uptime
free -h
df -h
```

Check Docker service:

```bash
sudo systemctl status docker
```

Check running containers:

```bash
sudo docker ps -a
```

Check Kafka logs:

```bash
sudo docker logs --tail 100 kafka-day1
```

Check Kafka port on the host:

```bash
sudo ss -lntp | grep 9092
```

Check Kafka topics:

```bash
sudo docker exec kafka-day1 \
/opt/kafka/bin/kafka-topics.sh \
--bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
--list
```

Check consumer groups:

```bash
sudo docker exec kafka-day1 \
/opt/kafka/bin/kafka-consumer-groups.sh \
--bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
--list
```

Note:

A successful port-listening check does not, by itself, prove Kafka is healthy.

Verify with a Kafka CLI request as well.

