PART F – TRAINER TEACHING SCRIPT

21. TRAINER SCRIPT

Welcome to Apache Kafka Intermediate Training.

Over the next nine days, we will understand Kafka architecture, producers, consumers, partitions, offsets, stream processing, Kafka Connect, security, and monitoring.

We will not only study theory. Every day, we will implement concepts practically.

Today, we will start by understanding why Kafka is used, how it works internally, and how to deploy and use Kafka on an AWS EC2 Ubuntu server.

At the Architecture Section

Think of Kafka as a central event-streaming platform.

A producer publishes events into topics.

Kafka stores those events in partitions managed by brokers.

Consumers read the events and perform their business operations.

Kafka is useful because applications can communicate asynchronously, without every service requiring a direct connection to every other service.

Before the Hands-on Session

Now we will create our own Kafka environment.

We will launch an Ubuntu EC2 instance, install Docker, run Apache Kafka in KRaft mode, create a topic, send messages, and consume those messages.

By the end of the lab, everyone should be able to demonstrate a working producer-consumer flow independently.

During the Consumer Group Demonstration

Notice that consumers in the same group cooperate to process partitions.

When we add another consumer, Kafka can redistribute partition assignments.

This is called rebalancing.

If we have three partitions and four consumers, one consumer cannot receive a partition assignment for this topic.

This is why partition count matters when designing Kafka applications.

Closing

Today we covered Kafka fundamentals, architecture, producers, consumers, brokers, topics, partitions, offsets, and consumer groups.

We also deployed Kafka and tested message publishing and consumption.

Tomorrow we will go deeper into topic management, partitioning, producer configurations, message keys, and producer behavior.

